WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:09.690
The Islamic State of Iraq and Syria is a phenomenon
so terrible and shocking it seems impossible.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:15.070
It controls an area the size of the United
Kingdom, commits mass atrocities, and launches

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.200
terror attacks abroad.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.150
To understand ISIS, it helps to tell the story
of its rise.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.350
That story begins far away and many years
before ISIS existed.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:33.570
In 1979 the Soviet Union invades Afghanistan
to defend a puppet dictator against rebels.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.320
Young men from the MidEast flock to join the
rebels.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.620
Many see it as a religious struggle, and some
develop extremist views.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.110
Among them is a well-educated young Saudi
named Osama bin Laden.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:52.560
Also in Afghanistan is a semi-literate former
street thug from Jordan named Abu Musab Zarqawi.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.530
They do not get along, and never will, but
will create the groups we today know as al-Qaeda

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.040
and as ISIS.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.710
The Soviets withdraw in 1989 and the Arab
fighters return home.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:10.700
Bin Laden grows al-Qaeda into a global network,
to continue the struggle against Islam's enemies.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.450
Zarqawi forms his own group, but it fizzles.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.039
Both men later return to Afghanistan, now
ruled by the Taliban.

00:01:17.039 --> 00:01:24.259
On September 11, 2001, al-Qaeda attacks America
from its base there.

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:29.159
The US invades Afghanistan and bin Laden flees
to Pakistan.

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:34.450
Zarqawi, still obscure, flees to a remote
and lawless corner of Iraq.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.380
Two years later, the US does something that
will transform the Middle East and set the

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:47.579
stage for ISIS: it invades Iraq.

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:53.929
The Americans topple Saddam Hussein's secular,
Sunni dictatorship and disbands the Iraqi

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:54.959
army.

00:01:54.959 --> 00:02:00.619
Thousands of Sunni Iraqi soldiers, angry and
unemployed, join the insurgency.

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:05.439
Jihadist groups, also Sunni, see this as a
repeat of the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan,

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:07.840
and again flood in to fight.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.479
Zarqawi is among them.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:13.989
Zarqawi's group becomes Iraq's most ruthless.

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:19.349
He especially attacks Shia, Iraq's majority,
deliberately sparking a Sunni-Shia civil war.

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:23.459
By 2004, Zarqawi is a jihadist superstar.

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:28.260
Al-Qaeda, isolated and weakened, attempts
to bolster its image by forming an alliance

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.260
with Zarqawi's group, which becomes known
as al-Qaeda in Iraq.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:38.700
But in 2006, Iraq's Sunnis rise up against
Zarqawi, and the US kills him in an air strike.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.319
Over the next few years al-Qaeda in Iraq is
largely defeated.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:47.380
The Americans withdraw in 2011 from an Iraq
that finally looks stable.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:53.810
In 2011, the Arab Spring spreads across the
Middle East.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.810
In Syria, Bashar al-Assad cracks down violently
on protesters.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.109
They eventually fire back, leading to civil
war.

00:03:01.109 --> 00:03:03.430
Assad fears the world will intervene against
him.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:08.799
So he releases jihadists en masse from Syrian
prisons, tinging the rebels with extremism

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:11.379
and making it harder for foreigners to back
them.

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:16.450
Back in Iraq, what little remains of Zarqawi's
group is still allied with al-Qaeda but now

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.900
known as the Islamic State in Iraq.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.719
It's led by a bookish religious scholar who
calls himself Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi.

00:03:23.719 --> 00:03:30.150
In early 2012, Baghdadi sends a top deputy
to Syria to start a new al-Qaeda branch to

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.659
fight alongside the rebels: Jabhat al-Nusra.

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:35.620
Baghdadi also attacks a series of prisons
in Iraq.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.140
They free former jihadists and recruit new
ones, growing stronger.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:47.030
In April 2013, Baghdadi announces he is taking
control of all al-Qaeda-allied forces in Syria

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:48.030
and Iraq.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:53.160
His group expands into Syria, becoming the
Islamic State of Iraq and Syria, or ISIS.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:57.790
Al-Qaeda rejects Baghdadi's power grab and,
in 2014, formally exiles him.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:02.560
The two jihadist groups, long at odds, are
now at war.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.629
ISIS grows powerful in Syria, in part because
Assad tolerates its rise, which he does because

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:14.239
it divides his enemies within Syria, and foreign
powers are too focused on ISIS now to worry

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:15.579
about Assad.

00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:22.610
By June 2014, ISIS has built an army in Syria,
and it launches a military-style invasion

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:23.610
into Iraq.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.810
The Iraqi army, weakened by corruption, folds
with little fight.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:31.340
Many Sunnis are tired of their Shia-dominated
and increasingly authoritarian government,

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.930
and welcome or at least tolerate ISIS's arrival.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:39.920
Within days, ISIS controls a third of Iraq
and a big part of Syria.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.840
ISIS's goal is more audacious than anything
imagined by al-Qaeda: to revive the ancient

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.460
Caliphate and expand it to encompass all Muslims.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:53.509
It earnestly believes its holy war will then
bring about the apocalypse as foretold in

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:54.509
scripture.

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:59.210
Thousands of Muslims, mostly from the Middle
East and from Europe, flock to join the group.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:02.979
Some join for religious reasons, but many
are just disillusioned or angry, and feel

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:06.371
that ISIS offers them answers and a purpose.

00:05:06.371 --> 00:05:07.930
ISIS quickly overreaches.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:12.669
That August, it invades Kurdish territory
in Iraq and Syria, sparking counter-attacks

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:14.970
from better-organized Kurdish forces.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:20.180
It launches a genocide against Iraqi Yazidis
and murders the American journalist James

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:26.610
Foley on camera, outraging the world and provoking
an American-led air campaign against it.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.570
ISIS is unable to withstand the onslaught,
and loses more than a fifth of its territory.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:36.849
In response, it begins launching increasingly
spectacular terror attacks abroad: Kuwait

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:41.220
City, Sinai, Beirut, Sousse and then Paris.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:46.570
So-called lone wolves inspired by ISIS propaganda
also launch attacks, though they're often

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.300
amateurish and less effective.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.020
Eventually, ISIS will lose its state.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:56.360
It is simply too weak, has no allies or outside
funders, and is surrounded by enemies.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:02.621
But ISIS will respond by reverting to what
it was before, as AQI: an insurgency and a

00:06:02.621 --> 00:06:05.430
terror group still capable of horrifying violence.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:16.139
In that form, it could be around for years.

